md`# brain-issues

Issues of a Cloud Brain: work, bugs, feedback and security reports about the Brain and its services.

Two things are kept apart. The **record** is every event in one order, each naming the hash of the one before it and signed. The **view** is what is true now, made from the record by one function (\`issueApply\`) and made again at will (\`issue.rebuild\`).

The tracker's code is the mechanism: the chain, and the rule that an act goes ahead only when its installed guard is true. Which states an issue has, who may move it and what a move needs are a **policy**: data, installed by an event of the same record. Built 2026-10-10; the design and what it leaves out are in \`plan/cloud-brain-issues.md\`.`
// %%
viewof issuesView = issuesPanel({ client, owner: !!(session && session.owner), invalidation, joined: () => issuesJoinedAt(brainModules, fromBrain) })
// %%
// The hash of the Worker whose module this page has. `loaded` is the page's own record (fromBrain of cloud-brain):
// loadService writes it each time it makes the page's copy the same as a Worker's, at opening and after a deploy from
// this page. Until it has a row for the Worker, what the deployer listed as the page opened (brainModules).
// Null in a copy opened from a file, which asks no deployer.
issuesJoinedAt = (listed, loaded = {}, worker = "brain-x-issues") =>
  (Object.values(loaded || {}).find((l) => l && l.worker === worker) || {}).hash || (((listed && listed.modules) || []).find((m) => m.worker === worker) || {}).hash || null
// %%
md`The panel above is for the owner, signed in. It answers from a copy of the view that the page keeps, one for each Brain's address, in \`localStorage\` under \`brain_issues2 ADDRESS\` (\`brain_issues ADDRESS\` until 2026-10-12; a copy under that name is not read): one \`issue.snapshot\`, then \`issue.sync\` every \`wait\` milliseconds while the tab is visible.

- **For your attention**, above the filters: the open issues that wait for the owner. One waits when it has the label \`needs-owner\` or \`security\`, is at \`awaiting-approval\`, is of kind \`security\`, or is not shown to this caller. They are in four groups, in this order: **To approve** (at \`awaiting-approval\`), **To answer** (labelled \`needs-owner\`), **Security** (the rest that are read), **Not shown to this caller**; inside a group by id, so an issue does not move when something is said on it. The block shows one of them at a time (since 2026-10-11; before that every one was a row of the block, each event in a box seven lines high that scrolled). Its first line has **‹ back**, the place (\`2 of 5\`) and **forward ›**, disabled at the two ends, then the name of each group that has an issue with its count, the group of the shown issue in bold; with nothing waiting it reads "Nothing waits for you." and has no buttons. Under that line is the shown issue alone, each event whole, with no height limit. The issue shown stays the one shown while it waits, whatever comes to wait before it; when it stops waiting, the one that was after it is shown, or the last. The box of an issue that is not shown is kept out of the page, and has what was typed in it when the issue is shown again. \`panel.waiting()\` answers the ids in order, \`panel.shown()\` the id shown or \`null\`, and \`panel.scrub(id)\` shows an issue by id and answers the id shown; an id that does not wait changes nothing. \`panel.row(id)\` answers a waiting issue as its row has it, \`{ issue, group, ask, about, box }\`, or \`null\`. A row starts with the issue's id, then one line that says what is asked, then the title (which opens the issue), its state, kind, labels and the project it is part of. Under that is what the ask is about: for an approval the review that passed; for an answer the comment the label was put on with (the last one by whoever added the label, before the labelling) or the body of an issue opened with the label; then the last comment, or the last event when nothing else is shown (an event of type \`cost\` is passed over, since 2026-10-12: with no comment, the last event that is not one). Then one box and the buttons. A move's button is named for the move the owner would make, not for the state it leads to: **approve**, **reject** (the owner's \`owner-reject\`), **triage**, **close**; its tooltip names the state. The one that is asked comes first, in bold: **approve** on an approval, **Answer** on an answer. **Answer** sends the text of the box as a comment and then takes \`needs-owner\` off, two events for one press; where the comment is taken and the label is refused, the box is emptied and **take needs-owner off** is pressed again. After those, **Comment**, and **take needs-owner off** where the issue has that label. The text of the box is the reason of a move or the body of the comment, and the box is emptied when the service has taken it. A write that is refused, or that the network drops, leaves the text: where the press had taken the issue out of the block, the next one was shown meanwhile, and the issue of the press is shown again with the same box and its buttons can be pressed again. Until the service has answered a press, the buttons of that row are disabled, so a second press is not a second event; the box can still be typed in, and it keeps the caret when another caller's event draws its row again. A move that needs a reason is disabled until the box has text. A move the guard would refuse whatever the reason has no button there, nor has a swap, nor a move that needs refs: those are in the open issue, which the row's title opens and where the buttons have the same names. What is under a row is one \`issue.get\` for each listed issue, asked again when the issue changes. The record has no link that says one issue waits for another, so a row the owner cannot act on yet is not told apart.
- **The block as data**, for a module that cannot import this one (the page's, which this one imports from): \`issuesAttention(panel)\` answers \`{ shown, draft }\`, and the cell \`issues_attention\` adds that of the panel above to the plugin set \`"attention"\` of \`@tomlarkworthy/plugin-registry\` (since 2026-10-11; nothing reads it yet). \`shown()\` is \`null\` with nothing waiting or no owner signed in, else \`{ id, title, state, kind, group, ask, about, place, of }\`: \`group\` is \`approve\`, \`answer\`, \`security\` or \`hidden\`; \`ask\` the line of the group; \`about\` the whole text of what the ask is about (the passing review as "pass: …", the comment the label was put on with, or the body of an issue opened with the label), \`null\` for a security report, for an issue opened with the label and no body, and until \`issue.get\` has answered; \`place\` of \`of\` is the "2 of 5" of the first line. An issue not shown to this caller has \`title: null\` and \`ask: ""\`. \`draft(id, text)\` puts \`text\` in the box of that issue, arms its buttons as typing does, shows the issue and answers \`{ id, drafted: true }\`. It calls no method of the service: the text is sent by a press, as a typed one is. Line breaks in \`text\` become spaces, since the box is one line. It throws, and changes nothing, for an id that does not wait (an issue a press has taken out of the block and whose write is on its way among them), for an issue with no box, for a text that is not a string or is empty, and for a box that has any text already, with that text in the message: what was typed is not written over. It shows the issue whichever one was shown, so a draft made while the owner types in another issue's box takes that box out of the page with its text kept.
- **State, Kind, Find** filter the table from the copy, with no call. State starts at **open work**: every issue whose workflow does not list its state under \`closed\` (in the default policy that leaves out \`done\`, \`closed\`, \`rejected\` and \`reverted\`), so a state a later policy adds is in it unless that policy closes it. **every issue** is the whole copy; after it, each state that an issue of the copy is at. Neither text can be a state's name, which has no space. The choice is not kept over a reload. The line under the table counts the whole copy, not the rows shown. Find reads the title, \`about\`, the labels and the id.
- **A row** opens that issue: its body, its links (what it says of other issues, and what they say of it; each opens that issue), its children, its events, and a button for each state it can move to and each swap its workflow lists. A button the guard would refuse is disabled, and its tooltip is the refusal. Reason and Refs are sent with a move, so a button can come alive as they are typed. Since 2026-10-12 an issue that has a run (\`issue.cost\`) has one line under its links, the totals of its runs from the row (\`issueCost\`): \`cost 3 runs · 1,820 s · 2 with a usage: in 122, written 283,746, read 7,122,698, out 35,770, 58 requests · advisor 4 calls · $0.3 of 2 runs\`. The five sums are of the runs that have a \`usage\`, and read \`no usage\` when none has; the advisor's calls are left out at 0; the dollars are the sum of what runs reported of themselves and are left out when none did. An issue with no run has no such line. An event of type \`cost\` is a line of the events: \`implementer round 2, resumed, in-review · 410 s · context 201,310 · billed in 61, written 141,873, read 3,561,349, out 17,885, 29 requests · advisor 2 calls\`, with \`no usage\` in place of the sums for a run sent without one, then \`$N\` when the run reported one and \`replaces N\` when it took the place of the run of event N. \`tools\`, \`event\`, \`of\`, \`agent\`, \`session\`, \`token\` and \`backfilled\` of a run are not drawn, nor the totals by role: \`issue.get\` and \`issue.runs\` answer them.
- **Refs**: entries separated by commas. \`kind:value\` is a ref of that kind (\`commit:abc\`, \`deploy:brain-x-a@hash\`); an address (\`https://…\`) or a text with no colon is a \`url\`.
- **Review** sends the verdict with the Comment text and the Refs. **Comment** and **Add label** each send their own. The **×** after a label of the open issue removes that label; where the label guard refuses (\`major\`, for anyone but the owner present) it is disabled and its tooltip is the refusal. An attachment of the open issue is a line under its body: its name, type and size. The name asks \`issue.attachment\` and saves the bytes as a file of that name; it is not opened in the page. A second press before the answer asks nothing. \`issue.narrow\`, \`issue.link\` and \`issue.attach\` have no control: each is a call.
- **New issue** opens one with an id the page makes. **Refresh** asks for the events since the copy now.
- **A newer panel is deployed**, under Refresh, with **Reload**: the page added this module once, when it opened, and a deploy of \`brain-x-issues\` since then is not on it. The panel asks \`getInfo?worker=brain-x-issues\` for the hash that runs when it is made, on Refresh, when the tab is shown again and when the window gets the focus, and compares it with the hash of what the page has: the page's own record of the module it loaded from the Worker (\`fromBrain\` of \`@tomlarkworthy/cloud-brain\`, written again when this page deploys the Worker), and the hash the deployer listed when the page opened until there is one. The line has both hashes. Nothing is said while they agree, nor when the call fails. A tab shown again asks twice (both events arrive); of calls on their way together, the one made last decides. Reload loads the page again; what is typed in a box is lost.
- **The last line** is the policy in force: the event that installed it and the \`reason\` it was installed with. A policy with no reason reads "the default" when it is event 1, and "installed with no reason recorded" otherwise (an install from before a reason was required). Earlier policies are not listed; \`issue.sync\` has them.
- A write is drawn before the service answers, and withdrawn with the refusal if it refuses.

\`issuesPanel({ client, owner, by, store, every, invalidation, joined, worker, reload, listen })\`: \`by\` is who the page judges guards as (the owner's session if left out); \`store\` is \`{ get, set }\` in place of \`localStorage\`; \`every\` is a poll interval that overrides the service's; \`invalidation\` stops the poll; \`joined\` is the hash of \`worker\` that the page has its module from, or a function that answers it, called at each comparison (with none, nothing is compared); \`reload\` stands in for loading the page again; \`listen: false\` makes a panel that asks on Refresh and \`check()\` only; \`save(name, bytes)\` stands in for saving an attachment as a file. Its value is the view, and it sends \`input\` when the view changes.`
// %%
md`## Methods

| | |
|---|---|
| Worker | \`brain-x-issues\` |
| \`issue.open\` | procedure. \`{ key, title, body, kind, about, parent, labels, id }\`. \`kind\` is one the policy names (\`bug\`, \`task\`, \`feedback\`, \`security\`, \`feature\`, \`project\` in the default). \`about\` is \`brain\` or a Worker's name. \`id\` may be made by the caller (\`a-z 0-9 -\`, 40 at most, not \`i<number>\`); left out it is \`i<seq>\`. A subtask is an issue with a \`parent\`. |
| \`issue.link\` | procedure. \`{ key, id, to, rel, remove }\`: issue \`id\` says \`rel\` of issue \`to\`, both of which the caller may read. \`rel\`: \`duplicate\`, \`cause\`, \`related\`, and \`part-of\`. \`duplicate\`: \`id\` is the same report as \`to\`. \`cause\`: \`to\` is why \`id\` happens. The first three are between two issues read by the same rule (the same \`read\` guard: a link is read with either issue, so one to a security report from an issue everyone reads is refused), and are kept on both issues: \`links: [{ rel, to }]\` on the one that says it and \`linked: [{ rel, id }]\` on the other, and neither changes what either may do unless a guard reads \`issue.links\`. \`part-of\` sets the \`parent\` of \`id\` to \`to\`, after it was opened: an issue has one parent, the new one replaces the old, and an issue is not made part of one of its own parts. \`remove: true\` takes the link off (404 when it is not there, 400 when \`remove\` is not a boolean); for \`part-of\` it needs no \`to\` and leaves the issue with no parent. A swap that changes who reads an issue drops its \`duplicate\`, \`cause\` and \`related\` links to each issue then read by another rule, from both issues, with no event of its own: \`escalate\` to security does, and so does each swap of an install's \`migrate\`, which puts back those whose two ends it brings under one rule (\`issue.install\`). Guard: the workflow's \`link\`, and \`label\` where a workflow has none. |
| \`issue.comment\` | procedure. \`{ key, id, body }\` |
| \`issue.label\` | procedure. \`{ key, id, add, remove }\`, lists of names |
| \`issue.attach\` | procedure. \`{ key, id, name, mimeType, text }\`, or \`base64\` in place of \`text\` for bytes that are not text: one of the two, not empty, 1048576 bytes at most. \`name\`: \`A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -\`, 100 characters at most, the first not a dot. \`mimeType\` is \`type/subtype\`, \`text/plain\` when left out; it is a label, and nothing is served by it. The bytes are kept by this Worker under the SHA-256 of them (\`blobs\`, key \`a/HASH\`), and the event is \`attached\` with the SHA-256 as \`sha256\` (an event's \`hash\` is its own), in \`{ name, mimeType, size, sha256 }\`: the record signs what was attached and does not hold it. The issue then lists it: \`attachments: [{ name, mimeType, size, sha256, seq }]\` in \`issue.get\`, \`issue.list\` and \`issue.snapshot\`. An issue has 20 at most, and a name once: a second attachment of a name the issue has is refused 403, so a later version takes another name. Nothing takes one off. Guard: the workflow's \`attach\`, and \`comment\` where a workflow has none. A caller the guard refuses, or who may not read the issue, leaves no bytes kept. |
| \`issue.attachment\` | query. \`?id=&name=\`. \`{ id, name, mimeType, size, sha256, seq, text }\` when the bytes are UTF-8, else \`base64\` in place of \`text\`. Read by whoever reads the issue; 404 \`NotFound\` for anyone else, as for a name the issue lacks. The answer is JSON whatever was attached, so no attachment is opened as a page of this Brain. The bytes are hashed again before they are answered: 500 \`BytesChanged\` when they are not the ones the record names, 404 \`BytesNotKept\` when the bucket has none. |
| \`issue.cost\` | procedure. What one run of an agent on the issue cost, as an event of type \`cost\` (since 2026-10-12; the design is \`plan/cloud-brain-run-cost-is-part-of-the-issue-model.md\`). \`{ key, id, role, round, how, outcome, seconds, context, tools, event, of, agent, session, token, usage, usd, backfilled }\`. Required: \`role\` and \`outcome\`, each a name, \`a-z 0-9 -\`, 30 at most; \`round\`, a whole number, 1 to 1000; \`how\`: \`fresh\`, \`resumed\`, \`light\` (spawned; sent to an agent that was there; run by \`agent.sh\`); \`seconds\` and \`context\` (the size of the run's last request, in tokens), whole numbers, 0 or more. Optional: \`tools\`, a whole number, 0 or more; \`event\` (the submit or the review the run made) and \`of\` (the comment the run was first written as), each the seq of an event before this one; \`agent\`, \`session\` and \`token\`, text of 1 to 100 characters; \`usage\`, all of \`{ input, cacheWrite, cacheRead, output, requests, advisor: { calls, input, output } }\`, whole numbers, and nothing else; \`usd\`, a number, 0 or more: what the process reported of itself; \`backfilled\`, \`true\`. A field that is missing, of another type (\`null\` is one) or not in this list is 400 \`InvalidRequest\` naming it, before the issue is looked for, and nothing is written; an \`event\` or \`of\` past the record's head is 403 \`Refused\`. A run is an issue, a \`role\` and a \`round\`. The issue lists one for each, in the order of their events: \`runs: [{ …the fields sent, seq, at }]\` in \`issue.get\`, \`issue.list\` and \`issue.snapshot\`; an issue with no \`cost\` event has no \`runs\`. A second event for a role and round the issue has takes that run's place, whole, and the service writes \`replaced\`, the seq of the event it replaces, into the new one: the usage of a run written without one, or a corrected number, is the same call again with every field, under another key. Both events stay in the record. The event moves the issue's \`seq\` and leaves its \`updated_at\`. Not checked: that \`event\` or \`of\` is an event of this issue, that \`round\` is the next, that a number is true, that a second event for a run was meant. Guard: the workflow's \`cost\`, and \`label\` where a workflow has none. |
| \`issue.review\` | procedure. \`{ key, id, verdict, body, refs }\`. \`verdict\`: \`pass\`, \`changes\`, \`block\`. What each does is the policy's: in the default, \`changes\` and \`block\` both let the issue be sent back, and a \`block\` also stops a pass until it is. |
| \`issue.move\` | procedure. \`{ key, id, to, reason, refs }\`. \`to\` is a state. Approval and revert are moves. |
| \`issue.swap\` | procedure. \`{ key, id, swap, reason }\`: a swap the issue's workflow lists. \`{ key, id, workflow, state, reason }\`: any other, the owner present. |
| \`issue.narrow\` | procedure. \`{ key, id, move, guard, reason }\` adds a guard to one move of one issue. \`{ key, id, remove, reason }\` removes guard number \`remove\`, the owner present. |
| \`issue.install\` | procedure, the owner present. \`{ key, policy, migrate, reason }\`. \`reason\`, required, text of 1 to 2000 characters, is kept in the policy event: who asked for the install and why. It is read back as \`policy.reason\` of \`issue.policy\` and \`issue.snapshot\` while that policy is in force, and in the policy event that \`issue.sync\` answers. Without one the answer is 400 and nothing is written. The default policy has none, nor has an install from before one was required (on cb4, the deploy of 2026-10-10 16:10 CEST); for those \`policy\` has no \`reason\`. \`migrate\`: \`{ HASH: { workflow, states: { old: new } } }\` moves the open issues of workflow \`HASH\`, each with its own event, keyed \`policy/<seq>/<id>\`. \`HASH\` is a workflow hash the record keeps (\`issue.policy\` answers those in force); anything else is 400 before the policy is written, and so is a \`states\` that names a state \`HASH\` lacks or maps one to something that is not a state of \`workflow\`, whether or not an issue is in it, and an entry with no \`workflow\`. An issue already on the workflow and in the state it would land in gets no event and is in neither list. With the view lost the answer is 503 \`ViewLost\`. A \`migrate\` that names a workflow the policy lacks, or would land an issue in a state its new workflow lacks, is refused with 400 before the policy is written. The issues are read again once the policy is written, and each again before its own event: one opened on \`HASH\` meanwhile is moved, and one closed or moved meanwhile is left where it is. \`{ seq, hashes, moved, left, relinked, unlinked }\`; \`left\` is \`[{ id, why }]\` of those not moved, empty when all were. A \`migrate\` under a policy that changes a \`read\` guard moves two linked issues one after the other, and the first swap drops their link (\`issue.link\`). After each swap the install writes again, as a link event keyed \`policy/<seq>/link/<n>\` and judged as any \`issue.link\` of the caller's, each link its swaps dropped whose two issues are now read by one rule: \`relinked\`, \`[{ id, rel, to }]\`. One that stays dropped is in \`unlinked\`, \`[{ id, rel, to, why }]\` with the refusal: its other issue is closed (a closed issue is not moved), was left, or is on a workflow read by another rule. Both are empty when the policy changes no \`read\` guard. The \`key\` is looked up before anything else in the body is read. One that a policy event holds, sent with the same policy, answers that event with \`duplicate: true\`, \`moved: []\`, \`left: []\`, \`relinked: []\`, \`unlinked: []\` and the \`reason\` that was kept, and moves nothing. The \`reason\` sent with it is not read: one that is missing is not answered 400, and one that differs is not written. A migration cut short is finished by the owner's own swaps. Sent with another policy it is 400, and so is a \`key\` another kind of event holds. |
| \`issue.policy\` | query. \`{ policy: { seq, kinds, names, reason }, workflows }\` in force; \`?hash=\` one workflow, an older one too. |
| \`issue.snapshot\` | query. \`{ seq, head, wait, policy, workflows, issues }\`: the whole view at \`seq\`. The \`body\` of a closed issue is left out (\`bodyLeftOut\`). An issue the caller may not read is \`{ id, kind, state, closed, parent, hidden: true }\`, here and in \`issue.list\` and \`issue.get\`. |
| \`issue.sync\` | query. \`?after=<seq>&head=<hash>&limit=200\`. \`{ seq, wait, events }\`: the events after \`after\`, and the milliseconds the page waits before it asks again. An event of an issue the caller may not read is \`{ seq, id, type, at, hash, hidden: true, issue }\`. \`reset: true\` and no events when \`after\` is past the record's head, or \`head\` is not the hash of event \`after\`: the caller's copy is of another record, and it starts again from a snapshot. |
| Settings | \`syncMs\` (5000): what \`issue.sync\` and \`issue.snapshot\` answer as \`wait\`. Changed with \`config.set\` on the core, no deploy. |
| \`issue.get\` | query. \`?id=\`. \`{ issue, children, events, cost }\`. \`cost\` is the totals of the issue's runs (\`issue.cost\`), from \`runs\` of the issue's row (since 2026-10-12): \`{ runs, seconds, billed, input, cacheWrite, cacheRead, output, requests, advisor: { calls, input, output }, usd, usdRuns, byRole }\`. \`runs\`: how many. \`seconds\`: their sum. \`billed\`: how many of the runs have a \`usage\`; the five sums and \`advisor\` are of those runs alone. \`usd\`: the sum of what the runs that have a \`usd\` reported, to a millionth, and \`usdRuns\` how many they are; both are left out when no run has one. \`context\` is not summed. \`byRole\`: the same totals for each role, \`{ implementer: { … }, reviewer: { … } }\`, in the order of each role's first run in \`runs\`. An issue with no run has \`cost.runs\` 0, every sum 0 and \`byRole: {}\`. For an issue the caller may not read the answer is \`{ issue, children: [], events: [] }\`, with no \`cost\`. |
| \`issue.runs\` | query, since 2026-10-12. \`?id=&role=&how=&round=&after=<seq>&limit=500\`. The runs (\`issue.cost\`) of every issue the caller may read: \`{ runs, total, more }\`. \`runs\`: \`[{ id, …the run as the issue's row has it }]\`, \`id\` the issue's, in the order of \`seq\`, the seq of the \`cost\` event in force for the run. Each filter is left out or left empty to ask for nothing. \`id\`: one issue; \`role\`: a name, \`a-z 0-9 -\`, 30 at most; \`how\`: one of \`fresh\`, \`resumed\`, \`light\`; \`round\`: \`first\` (round 1), \`later\` (any round after it) or a whole number, 1 to 1000; \`after\`: a whole number, 0 or more, and only the runs whose \`seq\` is past it are answered; \`limit\`: a whole number, 1 to 500, 500 when left out. A value that is none of those is 400 \`InvalidRequest\` naming the field: read as no filter it would answer every run. A name that is not one of the six is not read. \`total\`: the totals of every run that matches \`id\`, \`role\`, \`how\` and \`round\`, in the shape of \`cost\` of \`issue.get\` without \`byRole\`; \`after\` and \`limit\` do not change it, so it is the same on every page. \`more\`: runs past this page match; the next page is \`after=\` the \`seq\` of the last run read. A run written again between two pages has a new \`seq\` and is read again on a later page. An issue the caller may not read has no run in \`runs\` and none in \`total\`, and an \`id\` that names such an issue, or none, answers \`{ runs: [], total: { runs: 0, … }, more: false }\`. The rows are in the answer so that a median is the caller's to take. |
| \`issue.list\` | query. \`?state=&kind=&about=&parent=&label=&link=&limit=100\`. \`{ issues }\`, last changed first. \`link=ID\`: the issues that are part of \`ID\`, say a link of it, or have one said of them by it. |
| \`issue.verify\` | query. \`?after=<seq>\`. Checks \`seq\`, \`prev\`, \`hash\` and \`sig\` of each event after \`after\`. \`{ ok, seq, head, checked, guards }\`, or \`{ ok: false, seq, why, checked }\` at the first that fails; \`checked\` is how many passed. With \`guards=true\` it starts at 1 and also judges each event against the workflow in force when it was written. |
| \`issue.key\` | query, anyone. \`{ alg: "ES256", key }\`: the public key, a JWK. |
| \`issue.resign\` | procedure, the owner present. Signs every event's \`hash\` again with the key the Worker holds now, for after the key is replaced. No hash changes: \`sig\` is outside it. \`{ seq, signed }\`. |
| \`issue.rebuild\` | procedure, the owner present. Makes the view again from the record. \`{ seq, issues }\`. A write that lands while it runs is in the view it leaves: the head is read in the transaction that writes the last rows, and while it has moved the events after it are read and their rows written. After 6 writes with the head still moving it answers 503 \`Busy\`; send it again. Up to 297 rows the view is cleared and written in one transaction; past that in several, and a call between two of them is answered from part of the view |
| \`issue.lock\` | procedure. A lease on a name, for agents that must not do one thing at once (the loop's landing, \`land\`, and its deploy, \`cb4\`). Since 2026-10-11; the design is \`plan/cloud-brain-locks-held-in-the-cluster.md\`. Three bodies. **Take**, \`{ name, who, seconds }\`: \`{ held: true, name, who, n, taken, until }\`, and \`over: { who, until }\` beside them when the name was taken over a lock that had ended. A name that is held answers 409 \`Held\` with \`{ held: false, holder: { who, taken, until } }\` to every caller, the holder's own \`who\` and token among them. **Renew**, \`{ name, who, seconds, renew: true }\`: a new \`until\` for the caller's own lock, \`n\` and \`taken\` as they were; it never takes a name. A lock of the caller's that had ended and that nobody took since is renewed, with \`over\` naming the caller. A row that is another's answers 409 \`Held\` with \`holder\`; an emptied row or none, 409 \`NotHeld\` with \`holder: null\`. **Release**, \`{ name, who, release: true }\`: \`{ held: false, name, who, n, taken, ended, how: "released" }\`. By anyone but the holder it is 403 \`Refused\` with \`holder\`, for a lock that has ended too, and the lock stays; of a name nobody holds, 409 \`NotHeld\` with \`holder: null\`. \`{ name, release: true, force: true }\` releases whoever holds it, the owner present only (403 with no \`holder\` for anyone else), \`how: "forced"\`. \`name\`: \`a-z 0-9 -\`, 1 to 40. \`who\`: \`a-z A-Z 0-9 . _ -\`, 1 to 64. \`seconds\`: 5 to 1800, 600 when left out. The holder is \`who\` and the caller's actor (the delegation of a token's call, as \`caller.actor\` of a guard): the same \`who\` from another token is another's. \`taken\`, \`until\`, \`ended\` and \`now\` are milliseconds since 1970 by this Worker's clock; no caller's clock is read. \`n\` rises by one at each take of the name and is kept over a release, which empties the row and does not delete it. A lock past its \`until\` is not held: the next take gets the name. It takes no \`key\` and writes no event: \`issue.verify\` reads nothing of it. Nothing checks that the holder of a lock is the one who lands or deploys; it is an agreement between callers. |
| \`issue.locks\` | query. \`?history=N\`, 0 to 500, 0 when left out. \`{ now, locks, ended, history }\`. \`locks\`: \`[{ name, who, n, taken, until }]\`, held at \`now\`. \`ended\`: the same of each lock past its \`until\` that nobody has taken or released since, which is how a holder that died reads. A released name is in neither. \`history\`: \`[{ name, who, n, taken, ended, how }]\`, the last written first, a row for each lock that was released (\`how: "released"\`, \`ended\` the time of the release), forced (\`"forced"\`, the time of the call) or taken over after it ended (\`"expired"\`, \`ended\` its own \`until\`); the newest 500 kept. A lock that ended and was never taken again has no row there: it is in \`ended\`. |
| Storage | \`sql\`: \`issues_events\` (the record), \`issues_view\`, \`issues_meta\` (the policy and every workflow by hash); \`issues_locks\` (a row for each name ever taken) and \`issues_lock_history\`, made at the first call of \`issue.lock\` or \`issue.locks\` and read by no other method. \`blobs\`: \`a/HASH\`, the bytes of each attachment, once for the same bytes on two issues. \`issue.rebuild\` does not read them, and nothing deletes them. |
| Secret | \`ISSUES_SIGNING_KEY\`: an ECDSA P-256 private key as a JWK. Without it a write, \`issue.verify\` and \`issue.key\` answer 503 \`NotConfigured\`; the other queries answer. |

Every write takes a \`key\`. A key is kept once: a second write with it answers \`duplicate: true\`, with the first event when the same actor wrote it and with \`{ id, seq }\` alone for anyone else. A write answers \`{ id, seq, event }\`. A key that starts \`policy/\` is the service's own (the default policy's, and each swap of a migration) and is refused with 400.

**The signing key** is made by the owner, once, outside the Worker: \`crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: "ECDSA", namedCurve: "P-256" }, true, ["sign", "verify"])\`, the private key exported as a JWK, stored with \`secret.put\`, and let to this Worker alone with \`secret.setRule { name: "ISSUES_SIGNING_KEY", allow: 'caller.worker == "brain-x-issues"' }\`. Without the rule the Worker reads nothing and answers 503 (cb4, 2026-10-10). A refused act answers 403 \`Refused\` with the guard that refused it.

**Who can call**: the owner, a token and a Worker of the Brain. A member cannot yet (\`plan/cloud-brain-authority.md\` decides that).

## An event

\`\`\`
seq, key        its place in the record, and the caller's key
id, type        the issue; opened commented labelled linked attached cost reviewed moved swapped narrowed policy
workflow        the hash of the workflow the act was judged under
…               the act's own fields (title, body, to, reason, refs, verdict, …)
by, origin      { caller, via, holder, tab } as the core named them on this call, and the same for who started the chain
ray, at         the call's ray in Workers Logs, and the time in ms
prev            the hash of the event before it
hash            SHA-256 of the JSON of every field above, keys sorted, no whitespace
sig             ECDSA P-256 signature of hash (SHA-256), 64 bytes as base64url. Ed25519 until 2026-10-10: the cluster's browser is Chrome 128, which has none.
\`\`\`

A changed or removed event breaks \`prev\` of each one after it. What the chain does not show: the last events removed, or a whole new record written by whoever holds the key and the database. Nothing outside the Brain holds the head (Tom, 2026-10-10: a signed chain only).`
// %%
md`## A policy

\`\`\`js
{ kinds: {"bug":"task","security":"security"},
  workflows: {
    task: {
      states: ["open","done"],
      start: "open",
      closed: ["done"],
      read: "true",
      open: "true",
      comment: "true",
      label: "caller.kind in [\\"owner\\", \\"token\\"]",
      review: "false",
      narrow: "caller.kind in [\\"owner\\", \\"token\\"]",
      moves: [{"name":"finish","from":"open","to":"done","guard":"caller.kind in [\\"owner\\", \\"token\\"] && event.reason != \\"\\""}],
      swaps: [{"name":"escalate","to":{"workflow":"security","state":"open"},"guard":"caller.kind in [\\"owner\\", \\"token\\"] && \\"security\\" in issue.labels"}] },
    security: {
      states: ["open","done"],
      start: "open",
      closed: ["done"],
      read: "caller.present || caller.actor == issue.opener",
      open: "true",
      comment: "true",
      label: "caller.kind in [\\"owner\\", \\"token\\"]",
      review: "false",
      narrow: "caller.kind in [\\"owner\\", \\"token\\"]",
      moves: [{"name":"finish","from":"open","to":"done","guard":"caller.kind in [\\"owner\\", \\"token\\"] && event.reason != \\"\\""}],
      swaps: [] } } }
\`\`\`

That is the cell \`issuesExamplePolicy\`, and \`issue.install\` takes it as it is. A move's \`from\` is a state, a list of states, or \`"*"\`. A workflow may also have \`link\`, \`attach\` and \`cost\`: without them \`label\` guards a link, \`comment\` an attachment and \`label\` a cost. A guard is one CEL expression (*Access rules* in \`@tomlarkworthy/cloudflare-iac\`). It is true or the act is refused; an error refuses. A guard that does not parse refuses the install. A workflow is named in an event by the SHA-256 of its JSON, so an issue stays on the workflow it was opened under until a swap moves it.

| a guard reads | |
|---|---|
| \`caller.id\`, \`caller.kind\`, \`caller.via\`, \`caller.holder\`, \`caller.delegation\` | as a method's rule does |
| \`caller.actor\` | \`delegation:<id>\` for a call under a delegation (a token is one), or else \`caller.id\`. Two delegations of the owner are two actors. |
| \`caller.present\` | the owner, via \`session\`, in their own tab |
| \`issue.id\`, \`.kind\`, \`.about\`, \`.state\`, \`.labels\`, \`.parent\`, \`.opener\` | \`opener\` is an actor |
| \`issue.major\` | the issue has the label \`major\` |
| \`issue.by\` | a map: the name of a move → the actor who last made it. \`issue.by.start\` is the implementer in the default workflow. A move not made yet is an error, so the guard refuses. |
| \`issue.reviews\` | \`[{ actor, verdict }]\` since the issue came into its state |
| \`issue.links\` | \`[{ rel, to }]\`: what this issue says of others. Its parent is \`issue.parent\`. |
| \`issue.children.total\`, \`.open\` | issues whose \`parent\` this is, and those not in a closed state |
| \`event.to\`, \`.rel\`, \`.reason\`, \`.refs\`, \`.refKinds\`, \`.verdict\`, \`.add\`, \`.remove\` | the act asked for |

Every fact is in the record, so \`issue.verify?guards=true\` can judge every event again.

**Fixed in the code**, whatever the policy says: a caller the \`read\` guard refuses does nothing to the issue, opens no child of it, and is answered 404; installing a policy, a swap the workflow does not list, removing a guard of an issue and \`issue.rebuild\` need the owner present. The first write into an empty record, when it is not itself an \`issue.install\` and the default policy allows it, installs \`issuesDefaultPolicy\` as event 1, by this Worker. A first write the default would refuse is refused and writes nothing, and an owner who installs first writes event 1 themselves: the default is then never written. That is the one policy written without the owner, and it cannot be written again: a record with events and no policy in its view answers 503 \`ViewLost\` until \`issue.rebuild\`.

**Who is present.** No token is. Every token's call reaches a service as \`owner\` via \`delegation:<id>\`, the token of a sign-in link made with \`unattended\` among them: its cap \`session\` sets \`x-brain-tab\` and leaves the via as it is, and \`caller.present\` needs the via \`session\`. Read 2026-10-10 in the source cb4 runs (kernel \`fbc1a80ce059\`, core \`b68bebad811f\`); not tried with a link's token. What passes with nobody at a keyboard is a session minted from a Brain's state file (\`brain.ts curl --owner\`): 13 of the first 16 events on cb4 were made that way.

## The default policy

| workflow | kinds | |
|---|---|---|
| \`task\` | \`bug\`, \`task\` | open → triaged → ready → in-progress → in-review → awaiting-approval → done → closed → rejected → reverted. Listed swap \`escalate\` to \`security\` at \`triaged\`, for whoever may triage, when the issue has the label \`security\`. |
| \`security\` | \`security\` | as \`task\`, every issue needs approval, read by the owner present and its opener, no swap out |
| \`feedback\` | \`feedback\` | open → closed. Closed with what became of it: a reason, a parent or a link. Listed swap \`promote\` to \`task\`. |
| \`project\` | \`feature\`, \`project\` | open → spec → spec-review → awaiting-approval → approved → building → done; closed, rejected. Issues are made part of it with \`issue.link\` \`part-of\`, at any state: the reports gathered into it, and later the tasks that build it. |

Moves of \`task\` beside the straight path, each with a reason:

| move | | why it is there |
|---|---|---|
| \`start\` | open, triaged → in-progress | Whoever already has the fix starts with the reason triage would have given, in place of \`triage\`, \`ready\`, \`start\`. Refused for an issue labeled \`major\` or \`needs-owner\`, or with an open part. From \`ready\` it needs nothing: \`triage\` and \`ready\` are where whoever triages decides such an issue may be built. |
| \`back\` | in-progress → ready | The work stopped: it needs the owner, or its writer is gone. Until 2026-10-11 \`submit\` was the one move out. |
| \`close\` | open, triaged, ready, in-progress → closed | Ended with no change of its own: answered, a duplicate, gathered into a project, overtaken. \`rejected\` is for what is not real or will not be done. |
| \`reopen\` | closed, rejected → open | The evidence a reject asked for arrived. An issue the owner present has ever rejected (\`owner-reject\`, the name of any reject by the owner present) is opened again by the owner present only, for as long as it exists: \`issue.by\` keeps the name. |

A \`project\`: \`spec\` (someone takes it, with a reason), \`submit-spec\` by that actor with a ref to the spec, a \`pass\` review by another actor and \`to-approval\`, \`approve\` by the owner present (or \`send-back\` with a reason), \`decompose\` once it has an open part, \`finish\` when none is open, \`close\` with a reason from \`open\`, \`spec\`, \`approved\` or \`building\`. A \`changes\` or \`block\` review lets it be sent back to \`spec\` (\`respec\`).

Who may triage, label and narrow: the owner or a token; taking the label \`major\` off is the owner present. A reviewer and whoever passes the issue is not the implementer, and nobody passes it while a review says \`block\`. A guard added to one move of an issue does not stop another move to the same state: the owner's \`owner-reject\` goes ahead whatever is added to \`reject\`. \`approve\`, \`revert\` and a reject from any state that is not closed are the owner present. The guards are the cell \`issuesDefaultPolicy\`.`
// %%
// The smallest policy with a listed swap: two workflows of two states. Written out in the prose above, and installed by a test.
issuesExamplePolicy = {
  const team = 'caller.kind in ["owner", "token"]';
  const flow = (read, swaps) => ({ states: ["open", "done"], start: "open", closed: ["done"], read, open: "true", comment: "true", label: team, review: "false", narrow: team, moves: [{ name: "finish", from: "open", to: "done", guard: team + ' && event.reason != ""' }], swaps });
  return {
    kinds: { bug: "task", security: "security" },
    workflows: { task: flow("true", [{ name: "escalate", to: { workflow: "security", state: "open" }, guard: team + ' && "security" in issue.labels' }]), security: flow("caller.present || caller.actor == issue.opener", []) }
  };
}
// %%
issuesExampleText = {
  const j = JSON.stringify, w = issuesExamplePolicy.workflows;
  const flow = (f) => "{\n" + Object.entries(f).map(([k, v]) => `      ${k}: ${j(v)}`).join(",\n") + " }";
  return `{ kinds: ${j(issuesExamplePolicy.kinds)},\n  workflows: {\n${Object.keys(w).map((n) => `    ${n}: ${flow(w[n])}`).join(",\n")} } }`;
}
// %%
issues_service = cloudflare.Service("issues", issuesApp, {
  methods: Object.fromEntries(
    [["open", "procedure"], ["comment", "procedure"], ["label", "procedure"], ["link", "procedure"], ["attach", "procedure"], ["cost", "procedure"], ["review", "procedure"], ["move", "procedure"], ["swap", "procedure"], ["narrow", "procedure"], ["install", "procedure"], ["rebuild", "procedure"], ["resign", "procedure"], ["lock", "procedure"], ["locks", "query"], ["policy", "query"], ["snapshot", "query"], ["sync", "query"], ["get", "query"], ["runs", "query"], ["attachment", "query"], ["list", "query"], ["verify", "query"], ["key", "query"]].map(([name, type]) => [
      "com.lopecode.brain.issue." + name,
      // The owner, a token, a Worker of the Brain and the deployer, which opens an issue when it puts a Worker back
      // on its tests. A member's Worker calls as its author's DID and is not let in.
      name === "key" ? { type, who: "anyone" } : { type, allow: 'caller.kind in ["owner", "token", "worker", "deployer"]' }
    ])
  )
})
// %%
issues_announce = {
  plugins.add("workers", issues_service, { invalidation });
  return "announced issues_service";
}
// %%
issuesLimits = ({ rels: ["part-of", "duplicate", "cause", "related"], body: 20000, title: 300, reason: 2000, key: 100, refs: 20, labels: 20, attachment: 1048576, attachments: 20, policy: 100000, page: 500, syncMs: 5000, syncPage: 200, rebuildRounds: 5, verdicts: ["pass", "changes", "block"], lockName: 40, lockWho: 64, lockMin: 5, lockMax: 1800, lockSeconds: 600, lockHistory: 500, hows: ["fresh", "resumed", "light"], role: 30, outcome: 30, rounds: 1000, costText: 100, cost: ["role", "round", "how", "outcome", "seconds", "context", "tools", "event", "of", "agent", "session", "token", "usage", "usd", "backfilled"] })
// %%
// The guards of a Brain that has installed no policy. Written 2026-10-10 from the state table of plan/cloud-brain-issues.md.
issuesDefaultPolicy = {
  const triage = 'caller.kind in ["owner", "token"]';
  const said = `${triage} && event.reason != ""`;
  const other = "caller.actor != issue.by.start";
  // A block stands until the work is sent back: no pass beside it lets the issue through.
  const passed = `issue.reviews.exists(r, r.verdict == "pass" && r.actor != issue.by.start) && !issue.reviews.exists(r, r.verdict == "block") && ${other}`;
  // Taking the label major off an issue takes its approval away, so that is the owner present.
  const acts = { read: "true", open: "true", comment: "true", label: `${triage} && (!("major" in event.remove) || caller.present)`, link: triage, review: `issue.state == "in-review" && ${other}`, narrow: triage };
  const live = ["open", "triaged", "ready", "in-progress", "in-review", "awaiting-approval"];
  const states = [...live, "done", "closed", "rejected", "reverted"];
  // What the owner rejected is the owner's to open again. owner-reject is listed before reject, so the owner's reject
  // is recorded under that name from any state.
  const reopen = (from) => ({ name: "reopen", from, to: "open", guard: `${said} && (caller.present || !("owner-reject" in issue.by))` });
  const ownerReject = (from) => ({ name: "owner-reject", from, to: "rejected", guard: 'caller.present && event.reason != ""' });
  const moves = (always) => [
    ownerReject(live),
    { name: "triage", from: "open", to: "triaged", guard: said },
    { name: "reject", from: ["open", "triaged", "ready"], to: "rejected", guard: said },
    { name: "ready", from: "triaged", to: "ready", guard: `${triage} && issue.children.open == 0` },
    // From ready by anyone. From open or triaged by whoever already has the fix, with the reason triage would have
    // given: not an issue that is major or waits for the owner.
    { name: "start", from: ["open", "triaged", "ready"], to: "in-progress", guard: `issue.state == "ready" || (${said} && issue.children.open == 0 && !issue.major && !("needs-owner" in issue.labels))` },
    { name: "back", from: "in-progress", to: "ready", guard: said },
    { name: "submit", from: "in-progress", to: "in-review", guard: "caller.actor == issue.by.start && size(event.refs) > 0 && issue.children.open == 0" },
    ...(always ? [] : [{ name: "pass", from: "in-review", to: "done", guard: `!issue.major && ${passed}` }]),
    { name: "to-approval", from: "in-review", to: "awaiting-approval", guard: always ? passed : `issue.major && ${passed}` },
    { name: "rework", from: "in-review", to: "in-progress", guard: `issue.reviews.exists(r, r.verdict in ["changes", "block"]) && ${other}` },
    { name: "approve", from: "awaiting-approval", to: "done", guard: "caller.present" },
    // Ended with no change of its own: answered, a duplicate, gathered into a project, overtaken.
    { name: "close", from: ["open", "triaged", "ready", "in-progress"], to: "closed", guard: said },
    reopen(["closed", "rejected"]),
    { name: "revert", from: "done", to: "reverted", guard: 'caller.present && event.reason != "" && size(event.refs) > 0' }
  ];
  const closed = ["done", "closed", "rejected", "reverted"];
  // A project: issues are gathered into it (issue.link, part-of) while it is open; then a spec, its review, the owner's
  // approval, tasks opened as its parts, and done when none of them is open.
  const writer = "caller.actor != issue.by.spec";
  const specLive = ["open", "spec", "spec-review", "awaiting-approval", "approved", "building"];
  const project = {
    states: [...specLive, "done", "closed", "rejected"], start: "open", closed: ["done", "closed", "rejected"], ...acts, review: `issue.state == "spec-review" && ${writer}`,
    moves: [
      ownerReject(specLive),
      { name: "spec", from: "open", to: "spec", guard: said },
      { name: "back", from: "spec", to: "open", guard: said },
      { name: "submit-spec", from: "spec", to: "spec-review", guard: "caller.actor == issue.by.spec && size(event.refs) > 0" },
      { name: "respec", from: "spec-review", to: "spec", guard: `issue.reviews.exists(r, r.verdict in ["changes", "block"]) && ${writer}` },
      { name: "to-approval", from: "spec-review", to: "awaiting-approval", guard: `issue.reviews.exists(r, r.verdict == "pass" && r.actor != issue.by.spec) && !issue.reviews.exists(r, r.verdict == "block") && ${writer}` },
      { name: "approve", from: "awaiting-approval", to: "approved", guard: "caller.present" },
      { name: "send-back", from: "awaiting-approval", to: "spec", guard: 'caller.present && event.reason != ""' },
      { name: "decompose", from: "approved", to: "building", guard: `${triage} && issue.children.open > 0` },
      { name: "finish", from: "building", to: "done", guard: `${triage} && issue.children.open == 0` },
      // Also once approved: a spec with nothing to build, or a build given up.
      { name: "close", from: ["open", "spec", "approved", "building"], to: "closed", guard: said },
      { name: "reject", from: ["open", "spec"], to: "rejected", guard: said },
      reopen(["closed", "rejected"])
    ],
    swaps: []
  };
  return {
    kinds: { bug: "task", task: "task", feedback: "feedback", security: "security", feature: "project", project: "project" },
    workflows: {
      task: { states, start: "open", closed, ...acts, moves: moves(false), swaps: [{ name: "escalate", to: { workflow: "security", state: "triaged" }, guard: `${triage} && "security" in issue.labels` }] },
      security: { states, start: "open", closed, ...acts, read: "caller.present || caller.actor == issue.opener", moves: moves(true), swaps: [] },
      // Feedback is closed with what became of it: a reason, or the project or issue it was gathered into.
      feedback: { states: ["open", "closed"], start: "open", closed: ["closed"], ...acts, moves: [{ name: "close", from: "open", to: "closed", guard: `${triage} && (event.reason != "" || issue.parent != "" || size(issue.links) > 0)` }, reopen("closed")], swaps: [{ name: "promote", to: { workflow: "task", state: "open" }, guard: triage }] },
      project
    }
  };
}
// %%
issuesSchema = [
  "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS issues_events (seq INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, key TEXT NOT NULL, id TEXT, type TEXT NOT NULL, at INTEGER NOT NULL, hash TEXT NOT NULL, event TEXT NOT NULL)",
  "CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS issues_events_key ON issues_events (key)",
  "CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS issues_events_id ON issues_events (id, seq)",
  "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS issues_view (id TEXT NOT NULL, state TEXT, kind TEXT, about TEXT, parent TEXT, workflow TEXT, closed INTEGER, updated INTEGER, issue TEXT NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (id)) WITHOUT ROWID",
  "CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS issues_view_parent ON issues_view (parent)",
  "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS issues_meta (k TEXT NOT NULL, v TEXT NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (k)) WITHOUT ROWID"
]
// %%
// The tables of issue.lock. A list of their own, made by issue.lock and issue.locks alone: every other method makes
// issuesSchema before it answers, so a database that refused one of these would stop the locks and not the record.
issuesLockSchema = [
  "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS issues_locks (name TEXT NOT NULL, who TEXT NOT NULL, actor TEXT NOT NULL, n INTEGER NOT NULL, taken INTEGER NOT NULL, until INTEGER NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (name)) WITHOUT ROWID",
  "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS issues_lock_history (h INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, who TEXT NOT NULL, n INTEGER NOT NULL, taken INTEGER NOT NULL, ended INTEGER NOT NULL, how TEXT NOT NULL)"
]
// %%
issuesSql = ({
  head: "SELECT seq, hash FROM issues_events ORDER BY seq DESC LIMIT 1",
  byKey: "SELECT event FROM issues_events WHERE key = ?1",
  insert: "INSERT INTO issues_events (seq, key, id, type, at, hash, event) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3, ?4, ?5, ?6, ?7)",
  after: "SELECT event FROM issues_events WHERE seq > ?1 ORDER BY seq LIMIT ?2",
  of: "SELECT event FROM issues_events WHERE id = ?1 ORDER BY seq",
  one: "SELECT event FROM issues_events WHERE seq = ?1",
  resign: "UPDATE issues_events SET event = ?2 WHERE seq = ?1 AND hash = ?3",
  meta: "SELECT k, v FROM issues_meta",
  putMeta: "REPLACE INTO issues_meta (k, v) VALUES (?1, ?2)",
  // The issue and its children, and the same of a second id (the parent of an issue being opened).
  near: "SELECT issue FROM issues_view WHERE id = ?1 OR parent = ?1 OR id = ?2 OR parent = ?2",
  all: "SELECT issue FROM issues_view",
  putIssue: "REPLACE INTO issues_view (id, state, kind, about, parent, workflow, closed, updated, issue) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3, ?4, ?5, ?6, ?7, ?8, ?9)",
  onWorkflow: "SELECT issue FROM issues_view WHERE workflow = ?1 AND closed = 0",
  clearView: "DELETE FROM issues_view",
  clearMeta: "DELETE FROM issues_meta",
  // issue.lock. A released row is emptied (who '', until 0) and kept for its n. ?N are dense: D1 binds as many as it counts.
  lockOne: "SELECT name, who, actor, n, taken, until FROM issues_locks WHERE name = ?1",
  lockAll: "SELECT name, who, n, taken, until FROM issues_locks",
  // A take, in this order. ?2 of the first and ?4 of the second are now: a lock past its until goes to the history and
  // the name to the caller; an emptied row is past every now and has no history to write.
  lockExpired: "INSERT INTO issues_lock_history (name, who, n, taken, ended, how) SELECT name, who, n, taken, until, 'expired' FROM issues_locks WHERE name = ?1 AND until < ?2 AND who != ''",
  lockTakeOver: "UPDATE issues_locks SET who = ?2, actor = ?3, n = n + 1, taken = ?4, until = ?5 WHERE name = ?1 AND until < ?4",
  lockTakeNew: "INSERT OR IGNORE INTO issues_locks (name, who, actor, n, taken, until) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3, 1, ?4, ?5)",
  lockRenew: "UPDATE issues_locks SET until = ?4 WHERE name = ?1 AND who = ?2 AND actor = ?3",
  lockReleased: "INSERT INTO issues_lock_history (name, who, n, taken, ended, how) SELECT name, who, n, taken, ?4, 'released' FROM issues_locks WHERE name = ?1 AND who = ?2 AND actor = ?3",
  lockRelease: "UPDATE issues_locks SET who = '', actor = '', until = 0 WHERE name = ?1 AND who = ?2 AND actor = ?3",
  lockForced: "INSERT INTO issues_lock_history (name, who, n, taken, ended, how) SELECT name, who, n, taken, ?2, 'forced' FROM issues_locks WHERE name = ?1 AND who != ''",
  lockForce: "UPDATE issues_locks SET who = '', actor = '', until = 0 WHERE name = ?1 AND who != ''",
  lockHistory: "SELECT name, who, n, taken, ended, how FROM issues_lock_history ORDER BY h DESC LIMIT ?1",
  lockTrim: "DELETE FROM issues_lock_history WHERE h <= (SELECT MAX(h) FROM issues_lock_history) - ?1"
})
// %%
// The statements of one issue.lock call, and what their answers say: { batch, read }, with read(results) either
// { ok } or { refuse: [status, code, message, body] }. Apart from the app so that tests/tools/issue-lock-sql.test.ts
// runs the same batches on a SQLite; the test cells here run them on issuesFakeSql, which reads a WHERE as its
// writer did. A batch is one transaction on brain-db, so the read, the compare and the write are one step.
// A name is the caller's by this call when a write of it reports a change, never by what the row then says:
// the holder's own second take changes nothing and is refused as anyone's is.
issueLocking = {
  const S = issuesSql, keep = issuesLimits.lockHistory;
  const holder = (r) => (r && r.who ? { who: r.who, taken: r.taken, until: r.until } : null);
  const held = (r, was, now) => ({ ok: { held: true, name: r.name, who: r.who, n: r.n, taken: r.taken, until: r.until, ...(was && was.who && was.until < now ? { over: { who: was.who, until: was.until } } : {}) } });
  const none = (name, row, why) => ({ refuse: holder(row) ? [409, "Held", `${name} is held by ${row.who}${why}`, { held: false, holder: holder(row) }] : [409, "NotHeld", `${name} is not held${why}`, { held: false, holder: null }] });
  const ended = (was, now, how) => ({ ok: { held: false, name: was.name, who: was.who, n: was.n, taken: was.taken, ended: now, how } });
  return {
    take: ({ name, who, actor, now, until }) => ({
      batch: [[S.lockOne, name], [S.lockExpired, name, now], [S.lockTakeOver, name, who, actor, now, until], [S.lockTakeNew, name, who, actor, now, until], [S.lockOne, name], [S.lockTrim, keep]],
      read: (r) => (r[2].changes > 0 || r[3].changes > 0 ? held(r[4].rows[0], r[0].rows[0], now) : none(name, r[4].rows[0], ""))
    }),
    renew: ({ name, who, actor, now, until }) => ({
      batch: [[S.lockOne, name], [S.lockRenew, name, who, actor, until], [S.lockOne, name]],
      read: (r) => (r[1].changes > 0 ? held(r[2].rows[0], r[0].rows[0], now) : none(name, r[2].rows[0], "; not yours to renew"))
    }),
    release: ({ name, who, actor, now }) => ({
      batch: [[S.lockOne, name], [S.lockReleased, name, who, actor, now], [S.lockRelease, name, who, actor], [S.lockTrim, keep]],
      read: (r) => (r[2].changes > 0 ? ended(r[0].rows[0], now, "released") : holder(r[0].rows[0]) ? { refuse: [403, "Refused", `${name} is held by ${r[0].rows[0].who}, and not by this caller under the name ${who}; left where it is`, { holder: holder(r[0].rows[0]) }] } : none(name, null, "; nothing released"))
    }),
    force: ({ name, now }) => ({
      batch: [[S.lockOne, name], [S.lockForced, name, now], [S.lockForce, name], [S.lockTrim, keep]],
      read: (r) => (r[2].changes > 0 ? ended(r[0].rows[0], now, "forced") : none(name, null, "; nothing released"))
    }),
    // The rows of issues_locks as issue.locks answers them: held at now, or past their until with a holder still named.
    sort: (rows, now) => {
      const of = (r) => ({ name: r.name, who: r.who, n: r.n, taken: r.taken, until: r.until });
